.TH "HED-MAIL_GIT_PATCH" "1" "2026-08-11" "hed v1.19.0" "hed manual"
.SH NAME
hed\-mail_git_patch \- Turns git format\-patch output of the cwd repo
into a mail\-compose buffer so you can ship a commit to a maintainer the
kernel way.
.PP
Turns \f[CR]git format\-patch\f[R] output of the cwd repo into a
mail\-compose buffer so you can ship a commit to a maintainer the kernel
way.
.SH Commands
.IP
.EX
:mail\-git\-patch [range]      # outbound: compose a patch as an email
:mail\-git\-am    [args]       # inbound:  apply a patch email to cwd
.EE
.SS \f[CR]:mail\-git\-patch [range]\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
No arg → \f[CR]\-1 HEAD\f[R] (most recent commit).
.IP \[bu] 2
Anything else is passed verbatim to \f[CR]git format\-patch\f[R]:
\f[CR]:mail\-git\-patch \-3\f[R] (last three),
\f[CR]:mail\-git\-patch origin/main..HEAD\f[R] (the branch\[cq]s
commits), \f[CR]:mail\-git\-patch abc1234\f[R] (one specific commit).
.PP
If the range produces more than one patch only the first lands in the
compose; for a whole series, use \f[CR]git send\-email\f[R] outside hed.
.SH What happens
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]git rev\-parse \-\-is\-inside\-work\-tree\f[R] checks we\[cq]re in
a repo.
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]git format\-patch \-\-stdout <range>\f[R] runs in hed\[cq]s cwd.
.IP "3." 3
The leading \f[CR]From <sha> Mon Sep 17 \&...\f[R] mbox separator is
stripped.
.IP "4." 3
The patch\[cq]s \f[CR]From:\f[R] line is replaced with
\f[CR]mail_get_from()\f[R] (the address you registered via
\f[CR]mail_set_from\f[R] in \f[CR]config.c\f[R]), so msmtp\[cq]s
account\-by\-from\-header lookup matches the same account that
compose/reply/forward use.
.IP "5." 3
Empty \f[CR]To:\f[R] and \f[CR]Cc:\f[R] headers are injected.
.IP "6." 3
The rest (subject, commit message, diffstat, diff) is dropped into a
fresh \f[CR]mail\-compose\f[R] buffer with title \f[CR]Patch\f[R].
.PP
Fill in \f[CR]To:\f[R] and run \f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R] to send.
The recipient receives an RFC 822 message whose body is a
\f[CR]git am\f[R]\-applicable patch, exactly what
\f[CR]git format\-patch\f[R] + \f[CR]git send\-email\f[R] produces.
.SS \f[CR]:mail\-git\-am [args]\f[R]
The reverse direction.
Three call shapes:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]No args, viewing a patch email\f[R] \[em] pipes the current notmuch
thread as mbox into \f[CR]git am\f[R].
Works for a single patch email and for a thread that \f[I]is\f[R] a
patch series (the thread mbox contains every message in order;
\f[CR]git am\f[R] applies the patch ones and barfs on the others, which
is when you reach for \f[CR]\-\-abort\f[R] / \f[CR]\-\-skip\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]:mail\-git\-am \-\-3way\f[B] / \f[CB]\-\-continue\f[B] /
\f[CB]\-\-abort\f[B] / \f[CB]\-\-skip\f[B] / \f[CB]\-\-quit\f[B]\f[R]
\[em] flags forward to \f[CR]git am\f[R].
If the buffer is a mail\- message we still feed the notmuch mbox on
stdin; otherwise (e.g.
while you\[cq]re resolving a conflict in the worktree) \f[CR]git am\f[R]
runs standalone, so \f[CR]:mail\-git\-am \-\-continue\f[R] works from
any buffer.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]:mail\-git\-am /path/to/foo.patch\f[B]\f[R] \[em] apply a
file (or pass any other positional \f[CR]git am\f[R] argument).
.PP
The command hands the terminal off to \f[CR]git am\f[R] so you see its
progress and any conflict prompts; press Enter when it finishes to
return to hed.
Non\-zero exits leave a status hint pointing at \f[CR]\-\-continue\f[R]
/ \f[CR]\-\-abort\f[R].
.SH Dependencies
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]git\f[R] on \f[CR]$PATH\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[CR]mail\f[R] plugin must be loaded (the compose buffer +
\f[CR]:mail\-send\f[R] come from there).
